$schema: https://usm.dev/schema/v1.json
$id: smith-gray/capability-token
$type: feature
$version: 1
$last_updated: "2026-09-22"
summary: |
  The capability token is the only secret-bearing artefact an agent reasons about. A short-lived,
  scoped, audience-bound handle that contains zero secret material, it is safe to pass through
  prompts, memory, tool calls, and multi-agent buses where a value is not.
$system: smith-gray/system
$service: smith-gray/control-plane
status: planned
intent: |
  Agents must be able to define, create, transport, and request use of secrets without ever
  receiving plaintext or long-term keys. A token decouples "the right to use a secret" from "the
  secret" itself, so authority can travel through untrusted channels — model context, logs, MCP
  buses — while the value cannot.
flows:
  - id: mint-token
    name: Mint a capability token
    description: The control plane issues a signed token for a specific object, scope, and audience.
    steps:
      - id: s1
        action: receive
        target: Control plane receives an object id, requested scope, audience, and TTL from the sidecar.
      - id: s2
        action: validate
        target: >-
          Policy is evaluated — is this requester allowed to mint this scope for this object and
          audience?
      - id: s3
        action: generate
        target: Generate claims (sid, scope, aud, exp, nbf, jti) and sign with the active kid.
      - id: s4
        action: record
        target: Persist the jti so it can be revoked and audited.
      - id: s5
        action: receive
        target: Caller receives the token URI; no secret material is present in the response.
  - id: verify-token
    name: Verify a token at the sidecar
    description: The sidecar is the only component that validates a token before resolving.
    steps:
      - id: s1
        action: parse
        target: Parse the safekey:// URI and extract the token and claims.
      - id: s2
        action: validate
        target: Verify signature against the issuer's key (kid), then expiry (exp/nbf).
      - id: s3
        action: validate
        target: Verify the audience matches this resolver/environment identity.
      - id: s4
        action: validate
        target: Verify the requested operation is within the token's scope (least privilege).
      - id: s5
        action: validate
        target: Check the jti against the revocation denylist.
      - id: s6
        action: observe
        target: On any failure, deny by default without revealing why beyond a generic denial code.
contracts:
  - id: token-contains-no-secrets
    description: A capability token must never contain plaintext, key material, or anything that yields it.
    must_have:
      - >-
        Claims are limited to object identifier, scope, audience, time bounds, unique id, and
        signature
      - No field may carry the secret value, a DEK, or a KEK
      - >-
        A token captured from a prompt or log is not sufficient to decrypt a folder without a
        reachable sidecar and key authority
  - id: short-lived-and-scoped
    description: Tokens are least-privilege and short-lived by construction.
    must_have:
      - TTL measured in minutes to hours, never days
      - scope is one of read, unwrap, inject-env, inject-file, sign
      - aud binds the token to an intended resolver identity or environment
  - id: revocable
    description: Every token can be invalidated immediately, independent of its TTL.
    must_have:
      - A unique jti is issued and recorded at mint time
      - Revocation is enforced by a denylist checked on every resolve
      - Revocation takes effect without waiting for expiry
  - id: signature-verifiable
    description: The sidecar can verify authenticity offline of the issuing control plane's authority.
    must_have:
      - Tokens are signed and carry a key id
      - Signature verification happens before any key-store interaction
      - An unverifiable token is denied
tests:
  - id: token-has-no-secret-material
    flow: mint-token
    setup:
      secret_value: super-secret-value
    expect:
      - assertion: the minted token string does not contain the secret value in any encoding
      - assertion: decoding the token yields only the declared claims
      - assertion: no DEK or KEK identifier resolves to recoverable key bytes
    contracts:
      - token-contains-no-secrets
  - id: expired-token-denied
    flow: verify-token
    setup:
      ttl_seconds: 1
      wait_seconds: 2
    expect:
      - assertion: resolution is denied with a generic denial
      - assertion: no key-store unwrap is attempted
    contracts:
      - short-lived-and-scoped
  - id: wrong-audience-denied
    flow: verify-token
    setup:
      aud: env-production
      resolver_identity: env-staging
    expect:
      - assertion: resolution is denied
      - assertion: no plaintext is materialised
    contracts:
      - short-lived-and-scoped
  - id: revoked-token-denied-immediately
    flow: verify-token
    setup:
      revoke_before_use: true
      ttl_seconds: 3600
    expect:
      - assertion: resolution is denied even though the token has not expired
    contracts:
      - revocable
decisions:
  - id: uri-form
    decision: "Express tokens as a URI: safekey://v1/<id>?scope=<action>&aud=<env>&exp=<unix>&kid=<key-id>"
    rationale: >-
      A URI is copyable through any transport, self-describing, and easy to redact in logs by
      matching a prefix. It makes the token obviously a handle rather than a value.
    status: accepted
  - id: short-ttl-over-long-lived
    decision: Use short TTL plus a jti denylist rather than long-lived tokens with periodic rotation.
    rationale: >-
      Short TTL bounds the damage window of a leaked token; the denylist provides immediate
      revocation for the cases where waiting is unacceptable. Together they cover both the common
      and the urgent case.
    status: accepted
  - id: generic-denials
    decision: Denials return a generic failure rather than the specific violated check.
    rationale: Detailed denial reasons are a probing oracle for an attacker refining a forged token.
    status: accepted
  - id: jws-over-query-string
    decision: >-
      Carry the token as a standard RFC 7515 compact JWS in the URI fragment
      (safekey://v1/<sid>#<JWS>) rather than the blueprint's bespoke query-string claims with a
      separate sig claim.
    rationale: >-
      The blueprint's form is a bespoke signed-URL scheme: neither the canonicalisation of the
      signed bytes nor the parameter ordering/escaping is defined by any standard, so every
      implementation would have to reproduce our rules byte-for-byte and a mismatch in one language
      would be a silent authorisation bypass. JWS moves that risk into reviewed, widely implemented
      libraries. Decisively for this product, standardisation is what makes government-grade
      assurance reachable: FIPS 140-3 validated modules support EdDSA/ES256 but no private format,
      an auditor can verify RFC 7515 conformance quickly, third-party resolvers and HSMs
      interoperate on day one, and the format outlives the team. The blueprint's semantics (sid,
      scope, aud, exp, nbf, jti) are preserved exactly; only the signing envelope changes.
    alternatives:
      - option: >-
          Literal query-string form from the blueprint
          (safekey://v1/<id>?scope=…&aud=…&exp=…&kid=…&sig=…)
        rejected_because: >-
          Bespoke signed-URL construction with no standard covering canonicalisation or signing
          rules — we would own a crypto-critical parsing and signing surface. No FIPS story,
          expensive to audit, and a canonicalisation mismatch between implementations is a silent
          auth bypass.
      - option: JWS carried as a query parameter rather than a fragment
        rejected_because: >-
          Standard, but base64url-heavy JWS bloats the URL and risks truncation by intermediaries;
          the fragment also keeps the credential out of server logs and proxy records by
          construction.
      - option: Custom binary token format
        rejected_because: >-
          Maximum control at maximum cost: no library support, no interoperability, and an entirely
          novel format to audit.
    consequences: >-
      One deliberate deviation from the blueprint's documented wire format, recorded as an ADR.
      Implementations must enforce the alg allowlist and kid resolution, which are header-level
      concerns not expressible in the claims schema. Format is frozen at v1 and additive-only.
    status: accepted
    date: "2026-09-22"
  - id: alg-allowlist
    decision: Permit only EdDSA and ES256; reject alg 'none' and all symmetric algorithms (HS256/384/512).
    rationale: >-
      alg: none is the classic JWT attack, and accepting a symmetric algorithm lets a verifier be
      tricked into treating a published verification key as a shared secret (algorithm-confusion). A
      closed allowlist makes both classes of bug unreachable rather than merely discouraged. EdDSA
      is the modern default; ES256 is permitted for FIPS-oriented deployments.
    alternatives:
      - option: Trust the token's own alg header and verify accordingly
        rejected_because: >-
          This is precisely the algorithm-confusion vulnerability — the attacker chooses the
          verification algorithm.
      - option: Accept any asymmetric algorithm
        rejected_because: >-
          Unbounded risk surface, including weak or deprecated curves, with no way to reason about
          the whole set.
    consequences: >-
      Adding an algorithm is a new major format version. Header constraints are enforced by the
      verifier, not the claims schema, and are covered by header conformance vectors.
    status: accepted
    date: "2026-09-22"
  - id: key-custody-hsm
    decision: >-
      Require the token signing key to be HSM- or secure-element-backed from Phase 1; never a bare
      file in production.
    rationale: >-
      The token format protects the authority of the signature, so the signature's key is the root
      of that authority. A key on disk is exfiltratable by the same host compromise the rest of the
      architecture is designed to survive. Custody is where government-grade assurance actually
      originates — algorithm choice, key custody, and sidecar enforcement — not the envelope
      encoding.
    consequences: >-
      kid resolution must support an HSM-backed key source. No format change; only where the key
      lives. Recorded so an integrator does not ship a file-based signing key in production.
    status: accepted
    date: "2026-09-22"
implementation:
  primary: apps/control-plane/internal/token
  test_code: ""
  test_code_status: none
see_also:
  - smith-gray/secret-transport
  - smith-gray/token-revocation
  - smith-gray/sidecar-resolution
  - smith-gray/control-plane-db
